WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.003
Hey everybody.

00:00:01.003 --> 00:00:01.860
Welcome to the Podcast.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.230
My guest today is Bryan Fogel.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.450
Bryan is a filmmaker.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:09.030
He won an Oscar for his documentary "Icarus",

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.280
which if you have not seen it, you must.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.120
He was a guest on the podcast a couple of years ago.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.520
Bryan is back with a new film, it's called "The Dissident",

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.670
which is about the murder of journalist Jamal Khashoggi.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.220
It's extraordinary.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.420
This conversation is utterly compelling,

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.330
I think you guys are gonna really dig it.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.190
So hit that subscribe button and please enjoy

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:34.286
my conversation with filmmaker, Bryan Fogel.

00:00:41.446 --> 00:00:43.543
My name is Hatice Cengiz.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.433
I am addressing you as a victim.

00:00:47.706 --> 00:00:51.603
A title forced on me after the brutal murder of my Jamal.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.270
Jamal Khashoggi, prominent Saudi journalist

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:57.510
and Washington Post columnist has gone missing

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.150
after visiting his country's consulate in Istanbul.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:02.370
He was last seen entering

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.670
Saudi Arabia's consulate, taking paperwork

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.700
to marry his fiancee.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:08.980
His fiancee saw him go in at 1:00 PM

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.397
and was still waiting for him at 1:00 AM.

00:01:16.644 --> 00:01:18.650
Could you tell me what happened to Khashoggi?

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.920
Saudi Arabia, now suddenly there's admitting

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.587
that Khashoggi did die inside that building.

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:40.050
The government treated me as if I hurt the King.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.700
We knew that they would try to sweep the whole thing

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:44.867
under the rug.

00:01:46.329 --> 00:01:48.080
Is it true that Turkish intelligence

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.080
obtained audio recordings to Khashoggi's murder?

00:01:53.993 --> 00:01:56.530
I know why Jamal was killed.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:58.164
It's because of me.

00:02:16.443 --> 00:02:19.040
He saved some particularly damning piece of the puzzle

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:20.590
like Saudi body double.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.840
Jamal felt the whole country was against him.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.310
This is another truth.

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.500
I said the best solution

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.583
is creating our own army.

00:02:31.224 --> 00:02:33.915
The King firmly denies any knowledge of it.

00:02:33.915 --> 00:02:37.582
It could have been rogue killers, who knows?

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.900
I just received this, "Be careful,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.560
move from city to another one and there's a team

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.240
is going to kill you soon."

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.490
It's anonymous.

00:02:54.074 --> 00:02:56.710
He has to be killed in a way that will send a message

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:59.460
to everyone else, because the if you kill Jamal

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.923
with his papers, who else you cannot kill?

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.933
You can kill everyone.

00:03:11.193 --> 00:03:12.410
I (indistinct) Jamal.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.688
Once you starts working with us, you're not just

00:03:14.688 --> 00:03:17.438
a journalist, you're a dissident.

00:03:25.296 --> 00:03:29.254
What do you know about the bees?

00:03:37.225 --> 00:03:39.545
And a lot has happened in the last couple of years

00:03:39.545 --> 00:03:40.378
in your life.

00:03:40.378 --> 00:03:43.305
It's been a crazy trajectory for you.

00:03:43.305 --> 00:03:47.498
Yeah, it's been an interesting a few years.

00:03:47.498 --> 00:03:48.798
That's for sure.

00:03:49.645 --> 00:03:51.935
What was it like when you won the Oscar

00:03:51.935 --> 00:03:53.135
and you were up on that stage?

00:03:53.135 --> 00:03:56.055
I mean, that has to be one of the most surreal experiences

00:03:56.055 --> 00:03:57.028
you can imagine.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:03.180
It was March of 2018, and it was surreal,

00:04:06.275 --> 00:04:10.045
and what was interesting is leading up to that,

00:04:10.045 --> 00:04:14.695
the months up to it, "Icarus" released on Netflix

00:04:14.695 --> 00:04:16.535
in August of 2017.

00:04:16.535 --> 00:04:20.755
And the Academy awards was August, September,

00:04:20.755 --> 00:04:22.501
October, November, December, January, February, March,

00:04:22.501 --> 00:04:26.455
eight months later, seven months later.

00:04:26.455 --> 00:04:31.455
And Netflix had put a lot of energy behind essentially

00:04:33.415 --> 00:04:35.945
this award campaign and I had never went through

00:04:35.945 --> 00:04:40.945
anything like this before, and so it was seven months

00:04:41.925 --> 00:04:44.675
of every day of my life, you're waking up

00:04:44.675 --> 00:04:48.235
and here's your schedule for the day, here's your press,

00:04:48.235 --> 00:04:50.235
here's where you're going, here's your screenings,

00:04:50.235 --> 00:04:54.785
here's where you're going, and it was intense.

00:04:54.785 --> 00:04:59.745
And so, the lead up into that was months and months

00:04:59.745 --> 00:05:04.598
and months and months and months of this kind of campaign.

00:05:06.125 --> 00:05:08.238
And so I came into the...

00:05:09.815 --> 00:05:13.505
But from the time we get nominated to actually going

00:05:13.505 --> 00:05:17.975
to the Academy Awards and there I'm there sitting there,

00:05:17.975 --> 00:05:22.975
I mean, it felt like there was an elephant on me.

00:05:23.115 --> 00:05:25.895
I wasn't, at that point, having fun,

00:05:25.895 --> 00:05:29.705
'cause it was amazing, and at the same time,

00:05:29.705 --> 00:05:34.705
the pressure because there was show much feeling for me

00:05:36.835 --> 00:05:41.835
at stake and Netflix and my partners and everything

00:05:42.035 --> 00:05:43.915
had put so much into it.

00:05:43.915 --> 00:05:48.785
And so we were all there, kind of like on pins and needles,

00:05:48.785 --> 00:05:53.495
and luckily they called the award for Best Documentary,

00:05:53.495 --> 00:05:56.752
I think it was like the third or fourth award of the night.

00:05:56.752 --> 00:05:58.285
Yeah, it was early in the night.

00:05:58.285 --> 00:06:02.005
Which was really, really good because you're sitting there

00:06:02.005 --> 00:06:05.545
and they called "Icarus", and I just remember

00:06:06.655 --> 00:06:10.375
at that moment, I had essentially had left my body.

00:06:10.375 --> 00:06:15.132
I mean, it was just you were walking up on the stage there

00:06:15.132 --> 00:06:18.245
and there's this person, that person,

00:06:18.245 --> 00:06:19.705
everybody in the entertainment-

00:06:19.705 --> 00:06:22.425
That front row just looking up at you.

00:06:22.425 --> 00:06:25.035
You've ever admired and you're sitting there going like,

00:06:25.035 --> 00:06:28.755
wow, I'm on the stage at the Academy Awards

00:06:28.755 --> 00:06:31.455
with a Oscar in my head, and they handed me that Oscar

00:06:31.455 --> 00:06:32.675
and it's...

00:06:32.675 --> 00:06:35.245
I don't know how many pounds it is, it's like 12, 13 pounds

00:06:35.245 --> 00:06:37.235
or 10, I mean, it's heavy.

00:06:37.235 --> 00:06:39.412
I was like, whoa, I was like (laughs).

00:06:41.645 --> 00:06:46.645
And yeah, it was completely surreal, and an actor

00:06:48.097 --> 00:06:50.105
won the statue.

00:06:50.105 --> 00:06:54.775
You're led into this labyrinth underneath the Kodak Theater

00:06:54.775 --> 00:06:59.025
and you come out of the labyrinth about an hour later

00:06:59.025 --> 00:07:02.875
because they walk you through all these stations of photos

00:07:02.875 --> 00:07:05.375
and then a booth like, "What was it like to win the Oscar?"

00:07:05.375 --> 00:07:08.515
and all these kind of surreal things,

00:07:08.515 --> 00:07:11.385
and you emerge about an hour later,

00:07:11.385 --> 00:07:14.425
and as I kind of emerge out of the cavern

00:07:14.425 --> 00:07:19.068
about an hour later, right where the backstage is,

00:07:20.565 --> 00:07:24.495
is, can't remember if it is a Joel or Ethan Coen

00:07:24.495 --> 00:07:26.285
who's married to Frances McDormand.

00:07:26.285 --> 00:07:27.755
I think it's Joel.
Joel.

00:07:27.755 --> 00:07:32.755
So, Joel is there and Frances won the Academy Award

00:07:34.055 --> 00:07:38.978
that night for Best Actress, and he goes to me,

00:07:40.212 --> 00:07:42.206
"Enjoy this."

00:07:42.206 --> 00:07:43.656
And he goes, yeah, he's like,

00:07:44.819 --> 00:07:47.818
"Because it's like you never...

00:07:47.818 --> 00:07:51.028
He's like, "The odds are, this is never gonna happen again."

00:07:51.028 --> 00:07:52.320
(Bryan and Rich laugh)

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.645
And he also goes, he's like, and he tells me

00:07:56.715 --> 00:08:01.455
this crazy funny story about Sylvester Stallone

00:08:01.455 --> 00:08:03.875
that I won't repeat, and I just went on like,

00:08:03.875 --> 00:08:05.045
this is so surreal.

00:08:05.045 --> 00:08:10.045
I just won an Academy Award, I really should remember

00:08:10.875 --> 00:08:15.875
if it's Joel or Ethan Coen, talking to me like,

00:08:16.015 --> 00:08:18.228
they're my favorite filmmakers of all time,

00:08:19.475 --> 00:08:21.895
and, it was quite a night.

00:08:21.895 --> 00:08:22.728
It's pretty cool.

00:08:22.728 --> 00:08:23.905
And my parents were there too.

00:08:23.905 --> 00:08:25.575
Oh, that's nice.
Yeah.

00:08:25.575 --> 00:08:30.575
The campaigning that goes into the Oscar season is bananas.

00:08:30.585 --> 00:08:34.455
There's so much at stake for these companies.

00:08:34.455 --> 00:08:39.455
It is bananas and what's interesting is, in COVID now,

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:42.872
where it's so radically different because-

00:08:42.872 --> 00:08:44.635
Right, you can't press the flesh

00:08:44.635 --> 00:08:46.038
and go to all these lunches and all that kind of stuff.

00:08:46.038 --> 00:08:49.675
And all these screenings where with "Icarus"

00:08:49.675 --> 00:08:53.455
you had, I don't even know, 30, 40 screenings,

00:08:53.455 --> 00:08:57.315
were small screenings and you're meeting people

00:08:58.245 --> 00:09:01.795
and it was wonderful because I got to meet so many people,

00:09:01.795 --> 00:09:04.675
so many people that I looked up to or filmmakers

00:09:04.675 --> 00:09:07.248
or documentarians or you name it,

00:09:08.315 --> 00:09:10.665
and so COVID has really, really changed that,

00:09:10.665 --> 00:09:15.665
but it's really intense because there's a playbook,

00:09:17.515 --> 00:09:19.285
you've got your talking points

00:09:19.285 --> 00:09:22.775
and you've got the do's and the don'ts

00:09:22.775 --> 00:09:25.272
and what you're gonna say and what you shouldn't say

00:09:25.272 --> 00:09:29.458
and how to answer a question and everything was so,

00:09:32.155 --> 00:09:33.965
it was intense.
Yes.

00:09:33.965 --> 00:09:38.965
It was intense and the press I was doing,

00:09:38.995 --> 00:09:41.735
you then kind of like you'd go do an interview,

00:09:41.735 --> 00:09:43.352
and then after the interview, it was like,

00:09:43.352 --> 00:09:46.965
"Okay, that was really good, but maybe change

00:09:46.965 --> 00:09:49.644
the next time you answered the question, do this."

00:09:49.644 --> 00:09:51.285
(Bryan and Rich laugh)

00:09:51.285 --> 00:09:54.725
Well, thinking back to when you did the Podcast,

00:09:54.725 --> 00:09:58.385
I mean, you're definitely lighter in your shoes today

00:09:58.385 --> 00:09:59.858
than you were on that day.

00:10:00.775 --> 00:10:02.935
We had just met, so we didn't know each other really,

00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:05.915
but there was a little bit more of a seriousness

00:10:05.915 --> 00:10:07.705
and maybe a sense that you were shouldering

00:10:07.705 --> 00:10:10.625
that kind of heavy responsibility at the time.

00:10:10.625 --> 00:10:11.458
Yeah.

00:10:11.458 --> 00:10:14.695
I just thought it was interesting.

00:10:14.695 --> 00:10:16.915
I remember going through that so well,

00:10:16.915 --> 00:10:20.575
because I was enjoying it and the same time,

00:10:20.575 --> 00:10:24.225
I actually was I was so stressed out because each one

00:10:24.225 --> 00:10:27.065
of these events, you do these events and then it was like,

00:10:27.065 --> 00:10:29.045
okay, was that okay?

00:10:29.045 --> 00:10:32.505
And then I'd replay it in my mind and go,

00:10:32.505 --> 00:10:34.575
did I answer a question the right way,

00:10:34.575 --> 00:10:36.335
or did I say something that was wrong?

00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:39.225
Your publicist didn't call you and chew you out

00:10:39.225 --> 00:10:41.492
for, listen, if you're going on podcasts though

00:10:41.492 --> 00:10:43.528
and you're talking for a couple hours.

00:10:46.435 --> 00:10:51.435
I mean, the entire lead up to the Oscars

00:10:55.675 --> 00:10:57.825
and just that whole season, I mean, it was so intense

00:10:57.825 --> 00:11:02.475
and we had been nominated for a BAFTA.

00:11:02.475 --> 00:11:04.905
And so I'd been at the BAFTAs like two weeks before that,

00:11:04.905 --> 00:11:06.915
and we didn't win.

00:11:06.915 --> 00:11:11.255
And so you come out of the BAFTAs and here you're feeling

00:11:11.255 --> 00:11:15.115
completely defeated, but you've got the Oscar nomination

00:11:15.115 --> 00:11:16.652
and everybody at the time was going,

00:11:16.652 --> 00:11:21.652
"Well, don't worry about that, you still got the Oscars."

00:11:26.595 --> 00:11:31.595
But I mean, it was an incredible experience,

00:11:32.755 --> 00:11:35.405
but I can't tell you that I was having fun

00:11:37.347 --> 00:11:38.678
during that period.

00:11:40.545 --> 00:11:42.585
And part of it really had to do with

00:11:43.705 --> 00:11:48.705
what "Icarus" was about, and that here

00:11:48.865 --> 00:11:53.552
as this whole award season is going, and Grigory Rodchenkov,

00:11:55.498 --> 00:11:59.415
the whistleblower to this day is living in hiding,

00:11:59.415 --> 00:12:03.328
in protective custody, is isolated.

00:12:05.765 --> 00:12:09.635
And so what was weighing on me also, was that,

00:12:09.635 --> 00:12:12.775
okay, here I am having these experiences

00:12:12.775 --> 00:12:17.775
and I'm being celebrated and the guy who-

00:12:20.655 --> 00:12:23.875
Without him, that would not have been possible, yeah.

00:12:23.875 --> 00:12:26.715
This wouldn't have been possible without his evidence,

00:12:26.715 --> 00:12:30.025
without that story, none of this would be happening,

00:12:30.025 --> 00:12:35.025
is basically living an isolation in an undisclosed location,

00:12:35.295 --> 00:12:38.755
under security, under the threat of his life,

00:12:38.755 --> 00:12:43.755
and, here I am on this completely different trajectory now.

00:12:46.738 --> 00:12:51.205
And that really weighed on me, still weighs on me.

00:12:51.205 --> 00:12:53.145
Yeah, I mean, my first question, I mean,

00:12:53.145 --> 00:12:55.675
we're here to talk about "The Dissident", your new film,

00:12:55.675 --> 00:12:57.945
but we can't get into that without hearing

00:12:57.945 --> 00:13:00.855
a little bit about how Grigory is doing.

00:13:00.855 --> 00:13:02.665
Are you in communication with him?

00:13:02.665 --> 00:13:03.498
What's the latest?

00:13:03.498 --> 00:13:06.185
I know he put this book out recently.

00:13:06.185 --> 00:13:11.185
He put his book out, which is his memoir of his life.

00:13:16.585 --> 00:13:19.905
And actually, I can say this now because, by the time

00:13:19.905 --> 00:13:22.251
you hear this, it'll be known, it just won

00:13:22.251 --> 00:13:24.505
the William Hill Sports Book of the Year,

00:13:24.505 --> 00:13:28.055
which is a very prestigious prize in the UK.

00:13:28.055 --> 00:13:30.615
So he just won the basically British Sports Book

00:13:30.615 --> 00:13:35.615
of the Year award for the book, and yet the book

00:13:35.965 --> 00:13:39.245
still doesn't have a US publisher, which is bizarre.

00:13:39.245 --> 00:13:40.155
Yeah, I noticed that.

00:13:40.155 --> 00:13:41.655
He'd self-published it?

00:13:41.655 --> 00:13:45.745
No, it was published by Penguin Random House in the UK

00:13:45.745 --> 00:13:49.018
and then we couldn't get a US deal for it.

00:13:50.015 --> 00:13:53.985
I was involved just as no financial stake,

00:13:53.985 --> 00:13:56.908
nothing just trying to help facilitate,

00:13:58.185 --> 00:14:00.688
but the book's pretty amazing,

00:14:03.964 --> 00:14:07.225
and so that came out a couple months ago,

00:14:08.075 --> 00:14:13.075
but I'm not able to communicate directly with him.

00:14:14.905 --> 00:14:19.885
I don't have his phone number, I don't know where he lives.

00:14:19.885 --> 00:14:23.715
I don't wanna know where he lives, but we've been able

00:14:23.715 --> 00:14:28.715
to stay in touch through his lawyers, and he's doing okay

00:14:32.455 --> 00:14:37.455
but he hasn't seen his family since he escaped Russia,

00:14:40.145 --> 00:14:45.145
which was in November of 2015.

00:14:49.685 --> 00:14:54.685
He went into protective custody in July of 2016,

00:14:59.945 --> 00:15:04.945
so four years in, four and a half years in

00:15:05.708 --> 00:15:10.708
of essentially this guy living in isolation, in protection.

00:15:12.205 --> 00:15:15.032
And what's interesting is that the story

00:15:15.032 --> 00:15:17.168
has continued to evolve.

00:15:19.855 --> 00:15:22.165
When "Icarus" released in August, 2017,

00:15:22.165 --> 00:15:24.208
Russia was still going to the Olympics.

00:15:25.115 --> 00:15:28.155
Five months later, basically because of what

00:15:28.155 --> 00:15:31.715
that film's impact had been on the world at that point,

00:15:31.715 --> 00:15:35.398
and the global distribution that Netflix has,

00:15:36.235 --> 00:15:39.355
the Olympics, the IOC essentially had to do something.

00:15:39.355 --> 00:15:42.295
So even though at that point, the story had been

00:15:42.295 --> 00:15:45.825
in the press and the media, it's very different.

00:15:45.825 --> 00:15:47.895
Okay, you see something on CNN or read something

00:15:47.895 --> 00:15:49.695
in the New York Times versus now

00:15:49.695 --> 00:15:52.045
you're emotionally connecting to a character

00:15:52.045 --> 00:15:56.475
and you're seeing this in a film and you're realizing

00:15:56.475 --> 00:16:01.228
the extent of his fraud and kind of how bad it really was.

00:16:02.135 --> 00:16:05.995
the IOC decides to ban Russia

00:16:05.995 --> 00:16:09.505
from the PyeongChang Olympic games in 2018,

00:16:09.505 --> 00:16:13.105
and in the decision of the ban, even though the ban

00:16:13.105 --> 00:16:16.135
was largely ceremonial, because Russia was then able

00:16:16.135 --> 00:16:18.365
to still compete, but not under their own flag,

00:16:18.365 --> 00:16:19.725
and the athletes were competing

00:16:19.725 --> 00:16:22.258
as Olympic athletes from Russia,

00:16:23.245 --> 00:16:27.015
they cited "Icarus" in the reasoned decision.

00:16:27.015 --> 00:16:27.848
Wow.

00:16:27.848 --> 00:16:32.825
And so that was the Olympics, I think were February, 2018,

00:16:35.942 --> 00:16:40.942
and then at the time there was the, still WADA,

00:16:41.345 --> 00:16:44.695
The World Anti-Doping Agency had put together this roadmap,

00:16:44.695 --> 00:16:46.845
which was okay, here's the three things that Russia

00:16:46.845 --> 00:16:50.825
needs to do to get reinstated into world sport.

00:16:50.825 --> 00:16:54.235
And one of them was that they were supposed to provide

00:16:54.235 --> 00:16:59.235
the LIMS data, which is the Laboratory Management System

00:16:59.355 --> 00:17:03.822
data of everything they had been up to over the past decade,

00:17:04.925 --> 00:17:08.315
kind of thing, like, "Okay, we know you cheated,

00:17:08.315 --> 00:17:10.895
but you guys gotta come clean and you need to provide

00:17:10.895 --> 00:17:14.065
this data because they had believed that

00:17:14.965 --> 00:17:19.965
what was in this database was a ton

00:17:20.065 --> 00:17:23.055
of other doping violations and frauds

