00:34:54.888 --> 00:34:58.375
And then he realizes, basically he's fucked.

00:34:58.375 --> 00:35:02.265
And he catches a flight out of the Kingdom that night

00:35:02.265 --> 00:35:03.528
without telling anyone.

00:35:04.935 --> 00:35:06.395
He could go back at some point,

00:35:06.395 --> 00:35:09.398
but we all know he never did.

00:35:11.792 --> 00:35:16.645
And you see in the film, we show some old clips of him

00:35:16.645 --> 00:35:21.645
and you see him speaking in English and appearing

00:35:23.275 --> 00:35:26.405
on Western media speaking in English.

00:35:26.405 --> 00:35:30.785
And so he here is this guy who had spent his whole life

00:35:30.785 --> 00:35:33.398
back and forth between the West and Saudi Arabia,

00:35:35.265 --> 00:35:40.265
but he's in the Kingdom and he starts essentially

00:35:44.355 --> 00:35:49.355
speaking out, not in any sort of like aggressive way,

00:35:50.415 --> 00:35:53.332
but basically tweeting or writing,

00:35:53.332 --> 00:35:57.895
"Hey, I don't agree with this."

00:35:57.895 --> 00:36:00.575
or "Maybe this can be different."

00:36:00.575 --> 00:36:04.085
And this guy was listened to,

00:36:04.085 --> 00:36:07.485
he had 1.75 million followers on Twitter,

00:36:07.485 --> 00:36:11.735
which it was a big number, especially in Saudi Arabia.

00:36:11.735 --> 00:36:14.695
And so he was very respected.

00:36:14.695 --> 00:36:18.875
So unlike, let's say, a young up and comer or this, that,

00:36:18.875 --> 00:36:23.875
the other, this was now a very respected journalist,

00:36:27.575 --> 00:36:31.105
coming out with his thoughts or opinions and somebody

00:36:31.105 --> 00:36:32.895
who knew the inside workings.

00:36:32.895 --> 00:36:37.895
And so Mohammed bin Salman had kind of put together

00:36:37.985 --> 00:36:42.985
this whole, I guess, army as you'd call it, of people,

00:36:44.255 --> 00:36:49.075
to basically see to it that, there were no free speech

00:36:49.075 --> 00:36:51.895
or freedom of opinion in the Kingdom

00:36:51.895 --> 00:36:56.895
and Saud al-Qahtan who was one of the Royal advisers,

00:36:58.545 --> 00:37:00.895
basically apparently comes up with this plan

00:37:00.895 --> 00:37:04.685
of how they are going to see to it

00:37:05.804 --> 00:37:08.666
that there is no free speech in the Kingdom

00:37:08.666 --> 00:37:12.592
and Saud reaches out to Jamal and tells them,

00:37:12.592 --> 00:37:15.785
"Don't write, don't tweet, don't talk,

00:37:15.785 --> 00:37:18.015
you're to remain silent.

00:37:18.015 --> 00:37:23.015
And basically if you don't, we're gonna come

00:37:23.175 --> 00:37:24.008
and arrest you."

00:37:25.775 --> 00:37:30.775
And there's threats being made, and Jamal essentially

00:37:31.355 --> 00:37:34.575
makes a decision that he has to leave.

00:37:34.575 --> 00:37:39.495
He can't remain silent and this decision,

00:37:39.495 --> 00:37:44.495
we get into in the film, but he was married,

00:37:44.765 --> 00:37:46.955
he had children, his kids were living

00:37:46.955 --> 00:37:49.155
in the United States at the time.

00:37:49.155 --> 00:37:50.245
I didn't know about that.

00:37:50.245 --> 00:37:54.555
And, two of the kids were in the US one of the kids

00:37:54.555 --> 00:37:58.005
was in Saudi, was my understanding.

00:37:58.005 --> 00:38:03.005
And he was happily married and he makes this decision

00:38:05.435 --> 00:38:07.788
that he has to leave.

00:38:08.680 --> 00:38:11.265
And he has to leave, I think for two reasons,

00:38:11.265 --> 00:38:16.075
as I understand it, and his friends, who I got to know

00:38:17.315 --> 00:38:22.315
in the making of a film, is that the idea that he would

00:38:22.695 --> 00:38:26.283
sit there and be a lame duck and be silent,

00:38:26.283 --> 00:38:28.375
and that his whole life was as a writer,

00:38:28.375 --> 00:38:33.375
was as a journalist, that now his voice was silenced,

00:38:34.625 --> 00:38:39.625
was enough that it was so compelling that he felt that

00:38:40.195 --> 00:38:44.905
it was better to leave the country than to stay

00:38:44.905 --> 00:38:46.940
with his family and be silenced.

00:38:46.940 --> 00:38:51.940
And of course, I think, his family probably was not thrilled

00:38:52.885 --> 00:38:54.645
with that decision.

00:38:54.645 --> 00:38:59.645
And after he leaves he comes to Washington,

00:39:00.235 --> 00:39:05.235
he gets a job being a Global Opinions writer

00:39:05.645 --> 00:39:07.975
for the Washington Post.

00:39:07.975 --> 00:39:12.975
And, there's a story, and I certainly can't validate

00:39:13.605 --> 00:39:17.525
the truth of it for reasons,

00:39:17.525 --> 00:39:22.525
but apparently, the Saudis come to his family, to his wife

00:39:25.485 --> 00:39:30.485
and tell her that she has to divorce Jamal,

00:39:36.685 --> 00:39:40.365
because only a husband can grant a divorce from his wife

00:39:40.365 --> 00:39:43.955
and the Kingdom, women are not allowed to divorce,

00:39:43.955 --> 00:39:48.115
a man has to grant a woman the right to divorce,

00:39:48.115 --> 00:39:53.115
and apparently they brought her in and interrogated her

00:39:53.615 --> 00:39:57.165
and whatever, and said, "You're going to call your husband

00:39:57.165 --> 00:40:02.165
and tell him that he has to grant you a divorce."

00:40:04.394 --> 00:40:05.227
And he did.

00:40:07.927 --> 00:40:10.835
And he did because for her own good

00:40:12.987 --> 00:40:15.098
so that they would leave the family alone.

00:40:18.397 --> 00:40:21.465
And so he now was in Washington

00:40:21.465 --> 00:40:25.445
and he was essentially isolated.

00:40:25.445 --> 00:40:26.278
Yeah.

00:40:27.305 --> 00:40:31.765
And that began the rebuilding of his life,

00:40:31.765 --> 00:40:35.725
it began essentially, his ultimately finding

00:40:35.725 --> 00:40:40.725
of Hatice Cengiz, the woman that he decided

00:40:42.115 --> 00:40:46.135
that he was going to remarry, and that led to him

00:40:46.135 --> 00:40:51.135
going into the consulate in Istanbul where he was murdered,

00:40:51.155 --> 00:40:53.245
'cause he had went there to seek marriage papers,

00:40:53.245 --> 00:40:56.445
to prove that he was no longer married

00:40:56.445 --> 00:40:57.845
so that he can marry Hatice.

00:40:59.745 --> 00:41:00.925
It's devastating.

00:41:00.925 --> 00:41:04.215
The idea that he would leave his entire family

00:41:04.215 --> 00:41:06.518
behind knowing he would never see them again.

00:41:07.615 --> 00:41:11.352
Well, I think that he believed that...

00:41:13.185 --> 00:41:16.975
Well, the kids were in Virginia and Washington,

00:41:16.975 --> 00:41:18.235
two of them at the time.

00:41:18.235 --> 00:41:20.725
They are now back in Saudi Arabia,

00:41:20.725 --> 00:41:25.725
and I think they decided, in the aftermath of his murders,

00:41:26.785 --> 00:41:28.345
that basically, they had two choices,

00:41:28.345 --> 00:41:29.688
as I best understand it,

00:41:30.665 --> 00:41:34.475
choice one was fight for justice for their father,

00:41:34.475 --> 00:41:39.475
and the family would basically, never be able to travel,

00:41:43.115 --> 00:41:47.875
be arrested, be ruined, right?

00:41:47.875 --> 00:41:52.875
Because, how are you gonna take on this Kingdom?

00:41:53.135 --> 00:41:56.785
And you have so many cousins and relatives and aunts

00:41:56.785 --> 00:41:59.372
and uncles, I mean, so, so it was like,

00:41:59.372 --> 00:42:02.885
"Okay, either we're gonna go fight for our father's death,

00:42:02.885 --> 00:42:06.795
or we're gonna essentially accept a payout."

00:42:06.795 --> 00:42:08.965
There's stories that the payout

00:42:08.965 --> 00:42:13.965
was in the 10s of millions of dollars and remain silent

00:42:14.045 --> 00:42:16.595
and move on with our lives.

00:42:16.595 --> 00:42:19.798
And the family decided to go that route.

00:42:21.945 --> 00:42:23.855
I can understand that,

00:42:23.855 --> 00:42:27.215
because it wasn't just about the kids,

00:42:27.215 --> 00:42:31.155
it was our aunts, our uncle's, our sisters, our brothers,

00:42:31.155 --> 00:42:35.715
our cousins that, either we're going to...

00:42:39.005 --> 00:42:43.045
Whatever war we rage or fight and trying to fight

00:42:43.045 --> 00:42:46.435
for justice for our father is putting every member

00:42:46.435 --> 00:42:51.305
of our family in danger, so I think ultimately,

00:42:51.305 --> 00:42:54.668
the decision was made is okay, we'll go back to Saudi,

00:42:55.687 --> 00:42:58.445
we'll shut up, we'll move past this

00:42:58.445 --> 00:43:00.068
and we'll take the money.

00:43:01.265 --> 00:43:04.765
In the structure of the film, it's very interesting,

00:43:04.765 --> 00:43:06.235
the way you kind of set this up.

00:43:06.235 --> 00:43:10.565
You have these two protagonists that are essentially

00:43:10.565 --> 00:43:13.385
on a collision course with each other.

00:43:13.385 --> 00:43:16.495
You've got Khashoggi and all the events that led

00:43:16.495 --> 00:43:20.005
ultimately to his murder, but in parallel with that,

00:43:20.005 --> 00:43:23.705
there's this other guy, Omar, who's left the Kingdom

00:43:23.705 --> 00:43:28.705
and is living in Montreal and ultimately,

00:43:30.335 --> 00:43:33.855
these two cross paths on this kind of trajectory

00:43:33.855 --> 00:43:38.025
that Khashoggi is on from reformist journalist

00:43:38.025 --> 00:43:43.025
to becoming, essentially, a full blown dissident.

00:43:43.145 --> 00:43:45.315
So talk a little bit about how you structured the film,

00:43:45.315 --> 00:43:48.005
because it really does, I mean, I haven't even said

00:43:48.005 --> 00:43:50.315
this yet, but it's like, the film is extraordinary,

00:43:50.315 --> 00:43:53.475
you did an unbelievable job, it's so compelling.

00:43:53.475 --> 00:43:57.615
And it plays very much like a narrative thriller,

00:43:57.615 --> 00:43:59.905
like on some level, it's more like a Bourne movie

00:43:59.905 --> 00:44:04.135
than a documentary, like the score, all of the sound design,

00:44:04.135 --> 00:44:08.035
every element of it is keeping you on pins and needles

00:44:08.035 --> 00:44:10.225
the whole time, and there's like a Gestalt,

00:44:10.225 --> 00:44:14.015
a tempo to the movie that really is very hyper-engaging

00:44:14.015 --> 00:44:15.862
and unusual for a documentary.

00:44:15.862 --> 00:44:17.568
Well, thank you.

00:44:19.015 --> 00:44:24.015
That's certainly how we, myself and my creative partners

00:44:24.961 --> 00:44:29.961
wanted the film to feel, sound, look, structured.

00:44:33.155 --> 00:44:38.155
Is much like "Icarus", but in this heightened level,

00:44:38.465 --> 00:44:43.465
I looked at this story and said, okay, it's a thriller.

00:44:44.585 --> 00:44:47.848
It's all true, but this is a thriller.

00:44:48.946 --> 00:44:53.946
The murder, the story of why he's murdered,

00:44:54.165 --> 00:44:59.165
how he's murdered, all the forces that play, the characters,

00:45:00.628 --> 00:45:04.548
this is like a Borne film.

00:45:05.905 --> 00:45:09.595
And so there was a very intentional construction,

00:45:09.595 --> 00:45:13.335
cinematically, and how we structured the film,

00:45:13.335 --> 00:45:15.735
paste the film, put the film together,

00:45:15.735 --> 00:45:20.735
and used these cinematic techniques that you would see

00:45:21.345 --> 00:45:26.118
in "An Enemy of the State", or in those "Bourne" films,

00:45:27.662 --> 00:45:32.048
or, you name the kind of the spy thriller movie

00:45:33.095 --> 00:45:35.865
that were intentionally employed in the construction

00:45:35.865 --> 00:45:39.545
of this film, while remaining true to being a documentary,

00:45:39.545 --> 00:45:42.685
I mean, everything in the film, we shot,

00:45:42.685 --> 00:45:45.185
everything in the film was a fact, everything in the film

00:45:45.185 --> 00:45:48.055
is researched and archived and backed by evidence,

00:45:48.055 --> 00:45:53.055
but structurally, my feeling as a filmmaker

00:45:53.305 --> 00:45:58.105
and my collaborators, is I think, if you can engage

00:45:58.105 --> 00:46:03.105
an audience visually, through sound, music, motion graphics,

00:46:04.745 --> 00:46:07.375
effects, all these kind of devices,

00:46:07.375 --> 00:46:10.045
that when you're watching like a big thriller,

00:46:10.045 --> 00:46:12.195
gets you on the edge of your seat going,

00:46:12.195 --> 00:46:14.405
what's gonna happen next?

00:46:14.405 --> 00:46:17.385
And if you can do that, and especially in the case

00:46:17.385 --> 00:46:20.855
of this story, that hopefully the come away

00:46:20.855 --> 00:46:24.615
from watching, "The Dissident" isn't just that you're

00:46:24.615 --> 00:46:27.225
on the edge of your seat and you're having

00:46:27.225 --> 00:46:32.165
that kind of cinematic thriller esque experience,

00:46:32.165 --> 00:46:35.555
but that it also leads to a call to action,

00:46:35.555 --> 00:46:37.495
because you're emotionally impacted,

00:46:37.495 --> 00:46:40.955
because you didn't just watch a piece of news,

00:46:40.955 --> 00:46:44.145
you went on a journey, and that journey

00:46:44.145 --> 00:46:47.192
becomes very emotional, hopefully, in watching the film

00:46:47.192 --> 00:46:51.935
and that leads to what my intention would be

00:46:51.935 --> 00:46:55.205
and is that there's a call to action behind it,

00:46:55.205 --> 00:46:58.685
that you come out of the film and you fall in love

00:46:58.685 --> 00:47:01.165
with Hatice Cengiz, his fiance.

00:47:01.165 --> 00:47:04.405
You care about Omar Abdulaziz and that his brothers

00:47:04.405 --> 00:47:07.475
are still jailed in Saudi with no charges,

00:47:07.475 --> 00:47:09.315
and his friends are jailed in Saudi

00:47:09.315 --> 00:47:12.505
for two years without charges, and this guy

00:47:12.505 --> 00:47:16.815
lives in isolation, basically fighting the Kingdom

00:47:16.815 --> 00:47:18.228
for freedom of speech.

00:47:21.043 --> 00:47:23.815
You understand the truth behind this

00:47:23.815 --> 00:47:28.415
and want to do something about it, as you see the members

00:47:28.415 --> 00:47:30.985
of a G20 in the Trump administration essentially

00:47:30.985 --> 00:47:34.405
bury their head in the sand and condone this murder,

00:47:34.405 --> 00:47:35.888
all for all for money.

00:47:37.305 --> 00:47:41.115
And so, hopefully, in the construction of the film,

00:47:41.115 --> 00:47:44.505
being a thriller, that it also leads

00:47:44.505 --> 00:47:48.435
to a greater emotional response,

00:47:48.435 --> 00:47:50.035
and that was kind of the intention.

00:47:50.035 --> 00:47:51.005
Yeah.

00:47:51.005 --> 00:47:53.015
I mean, I knew the story.

00:47:53.015 --> 00:47:56.535
I had read some of his pieces in in the Washington Post.

00:47:56.535 --> 00:48:00.985
I had a familiarity with kind of the broader brushstrokes

00:48:00.985 --> 00:48:02.635
of what had transpired.

00:48:02.635 --> 00:48:07.105
What I was not aware of was one, focus of the film

00:48:08.025 --> 00:48:11.475
being on on the extent of their surveillance state,

00:48:11.475 --> 00:48:16.065
and how the Pegasus malware, and the hacking of the phones,

00:48:16.065 --> 00:48:18.195
and how all of that, like played into

00:48:18.195 --> 00:48:21.094
how that how this whole thing unfolded.

00:48:21.094 --> 00:48:25.822
To back up, Khashoggi enter the consulate on October 2nd,

00:48:31.565 --> 00:48:35.372
basically, two weeks later, I think it was the 16th,

00:48:37.165 --> 00:48:40.575
Saudi Arabia finally admitted that he had, in fact,

00:48:40.575 --> 00:48:43.375
died inside that consulate.

00:48:43.375 --> 00:48:46.485
And in the film, there's kind of the whole story

00:48:46.485 --> 00:48:51.325
of how that kind of unfolds, and the pressure

00:48:51.325 --> 00:48:54.685
that Turkey put on Saudi Arabia to get them

00:48:54.685 --> 00:48:57.858
to ultimately confess because they had the audio.

00:48:58.925 --> 00:49:02.855
And I had been following this story, essentially,

00:49:02.855 --> 00:49:04.705
from October 3.

00:49:04.705 --> 00:49:08.255
Washington Post journalist vanishes

00:49:08.255 --> 00:49:11.438
inside of Saudi consulate in Istanbul.

00:49:12.765 --> 00:49:16.475
And I'm reading this story, as I think many

00:49:16.475 --> 00:49:19.825
in the world began following this story in those two weeks,

00:49:19.825 --> 00:49:24.825
and, each day was another shocking allegation,

00:49:28.205 --> 00:49:33.205
and that he had been dismembered and that the body

00:49:33.245 --> 00:49:35.455
was nowhere to be found, and that they won't let them

00:49:35.455 --> 00:49:36.845
in the consulate and the-

00:49:36.845 --> 00:49:40.195
Met with crazier and crazier excuses

00:49:40.195 --> 00:49:42.925
on behalf of the Kingdom about what had happened.

00:49:42.925 --> 00:49:47.645
Yeah, and I'm reading this and I remember,

00:49:48.815 --> 00:49:53.815
'cause I'm pretty sure it was October 16th, 17th,

00:49:53.995 --> 00:49:58.995
and I was in Rome at the time, I had been invited

00:49:59.055 --> 00:50:01.428
to speak for the Rome Film Festival,

00:50:04.541 --> 00:50:09.541
and I was with my fiance and I'd been following the story,

00:50:10.005 --> 00:50:12.505
and as I started following that story, in those two weeks,

00:50:12.505 --> 00:50:17.295
in my mind, I was going, maybe this is the next film

00:50:17.295 --> 00:50:18.825
I wanna make.

00:50:18.825 --> 00:50:21.895
This seemed to have all these ingredients,

00:50:21.895 --> 00:50:26.375
'cause I had been looking, at that point, for about a year,

00:50:26.375 --> 00:50:29.865
for what that next story I wanted to take on,

00:50:29.865 --> 00:50:33.075
what that next documentary I wanted to make was,

00:50:33.075 --> 00:50:35.955
and I felt kind of a lot of pressure and burden

00:50:35.955 --> 00:50:38.245
coming out of "Icarus" that I wasn't gonna go

00:50:38.245 --> 00:50:42.148
and direct a Disney film, I couldn't go, whatever,

00:50:43.195 --> 00:50:46.208
make the Stevie Wonder documentary or whatever that was.

00:50:47.955 --> 00:50:50.375
I was gonna have to, at least in my mind,

00:50:50.375 --> 00:50:52.555
I wanted to see to it that I was taking on something

00:50:52.555 --> 00:50:56.925
that was human rights, freedom of press,

00:50:56.925 --> 00:51:00.305
freedom of journalism, protecting a whistleblower,

00:51:00.305 --> 00:51:02.945
authoritarian regimes, dictatorships,

00:51:02.945 --> 00:51:07.428
all these kind of themes that as a storyteller,

00:51:09.075 --> 00:51:14.075
I wanted to be able to continue in that path.

00:51:14.075 --> 00:51:18.975
And here was the story of a journalist,

00:51:18.975 --> 00:51:21.907
and not only that, he's Washington Post journalist,

00:51:21.907 --> 00:51:26.907
on his fighting for free speech, and he's advocating

00:51:27.065 --> 00:51:29.875
for human rights, and he disagrees

00:51:29.875 --> 00:51:33.755
with this authoritarian government, and he's at essentially

00:51:33.755 --> 00:51:37.375
a war of words with the Crown Prince,

00:51:37.375 --> 00:51:38.725
and now he's been murdered.

00:51:39.974 --> 00:51:44.225
And I said, okay, this could be the one and the question

00:51:44.225 --> 00:51:49.225
was, can I essentially get access to this story

00:51:51.815 --> 00:51:53.995
to be able to tell it in a manner that isn't going

00:51:53.995 --> 00:51:56.295
to be something that's archival?

00:51:56.295 --> 00:51:59.335
And there were three things that really hinged on him,

00:51:59.335 --> 00:52:03.635
which was one, there was the story of his fiancee

00:52:03.635 --> 00:52:08.395
emerging of the girl, Hatice Cengiz was waiting

00:52:08.395 --> 00:52:11.705
for him outside the consulate, and I said,

00:52:11.705 --> 00:52:16.345
oh, wow, this is clearly the emotional core

00:52:16.345 --> 00:52:17.865
of what this story is.

00:52:17.865 --> 00:52:21.845
I mean, the concept, I mean, which is unfathomable to me,

00:52:21.845 --> 00:52:25.285
it's unfathomable to anybody listening to this,

00:52:25.285 --> 00:52:28.935
that the person that you love, that you believe

00:52:28.935 --> 00:52:32.045
you're going to marry and spend your life with,

00:52:32.045 --> 00:52:35.698
walks into a consulate, an embassy,

00:52:36.935 --> 00:52:40.705
to be murdered horrifically, and that you're never

00:52:40.705 --> 00:52:42.805
going to see them again.

00:52:42.805 --> 00:52:47.465
And then at the same time, become, in Hatice's case,

00:52:47.465 --> 00:52:49.608
the center of a global media storm.

00:52:51.015 --> 00:52:54.565
And so all of a sudden, you become famous and famous

00:52:54.565 --> 00:52:56.705
around the world to the point where you can't really

00:52:56.705 --> 00:53:00.635
even leave your house, not because of something you did

