01:42:03.005 --> 01:42:07.535
And every one of the major global streamers, global media,

01:42:07.535 --> 01:42:11.405
entertainment companies, studios, that could have acquired

01:42:11.405 --> 01:42:15.228
and given life to this film, silence.

01:42:16.685 --> 01:42:21.685
And what we see here is essentially that the dollar,

01:42:23.565 --> 01:42:28.565
the Saudi investment, the potential to grow in that region,

01:42:30.025 --> 01:42:34.185
to gain subscribers into Saudi Arabian here,

01:42:34.185 --> 01:42:39.185
Netflix took off Hasan Minhaj's show for criticizing MBS.

01:42:39.645 --> 01:42:42.225
They pulled like a whole episode, right?

01:42:42.225 --> 01:42:44.585
They pulled out a whole episode, and just a few weeks ago,

01:42:44.585 --> 01:42:48.718
they announced an eight-slate deal with Saudi Arabia.

01:42:50.815 --> 01:42:53.925
Is Saudi Arabia invested in Netflix?

01:42:53.925 --> 01:42:56.185
Are they an equity partner in that company?

01:42:56.185 --> 01:43:01.185
I have no idea, but I think what we see and it speaks

01:43:02.665 --> 01:43:07.665
not just to Netflix, it's across the board,

01:43:08.825 --> 01:43:13.825
is that human rights and freedom of speech

01:43:13.955 --> 01:43:17.845
isn't just in danger and being suppressed

01:43:18.905 --> 01:43:23.555
in places like Saudi Arabia, it's here in the United States,

01:43:23.555 --> 01:43:27.235
where essentially, our economic interest,

01:43:27.235 --> 01:43:30.925
and these interests of these massive companies

01:43:30.925 --> 01:43:34.755
that have the ability to disseminate information,

01:43:34.755 --> 01:43:37.885
that have the ability for a global audience

01:43:37.885 --> 01:43:41.045
to see something and learn and take action

01:43:41.045 --> 01:43:43.305
and stand up against forces like this

01:43:43.305 --> 01:43:46.325
and bring about change, are choosing

01:43:46.325 --> 01:43:48.512
their business interests and economic interest

01:43:48.512 --> 01:43:53.355
and their shareholder interest, over what their audiences

01:43:53.355 --> 01:43:55.428
actually would want to see,

01:43:57.175 --> 01:44:00.842
or would want to see on top of seeing "Love is Blind".

01:44:02.245 --> 01:44:07.245
And so, we're in a moment here, where these companies

01:44:08.595 --> 01:44:10.945
have grown so big and so powerful,

01:44:10.945 --> 01:44:14.665
and we're in this huge, global landscape,

01:44:14.665 --> 01:44:17.105
whereas, years ago, you could go,

01:44:17.105 --> 01:44:21.045
okay, hey, you're a company, and you're just distributing

01:44:21.045 --> 01:44:23.655
the film to The United States, so you're like,

01:44:23.655 --> 01:44:26.705
okay, we can put this out in the United States, no problem.

01:44:26.705 --> 01:44:31.705
But now the formula is global and so nobody wants

01:44:32.135 --> 01:44:34.085
to put something out with the idea that,

01:44:34.085 --> 01:44:38.802
oh, you might upset China, you might upset Russia,

01:44:40.635 --> 01:44:43.575
you might upset Saudi, you might upset Egypt,

01:44:43.575 --> 01:44:46.905
you might upset Brazil, wherever you call it.

01:44:46.905 --> 01:44:50.605
And so the appetite for content that actually

01:44:50.605 --> 01:44:53.645
is taking on subject matters such as this,

01:44:53.645 --> 01:44:56.595
over the last couple years, has been completely

01:44:56.595 --> 01:45:01.595
and utterly diminished, and these companies

01:45:01.715 --> 01:45:05.785
are now operating much in the way of like,

01:45:05.785 --> 01:45:09.915
a government, where you're going, "Okay,

01:45:09.915 --> 01:45:13.859
do we punish the murder of Alexander Litvinenko?

01:45:13.859 --> 01:45:16.918
Well, if we do that, what are we doing?

01:45:16.918 --> 01:45:18.275
Are we starting a war with Russia?

01:45:18.275 --> 01:45:19.805
No, we're not gonna do that.

01:45:19.805 --> 01:45:21.705
Are we gonna stop doing business with Russia?

01:45:21.705 --> 01:45:22.882
No, we're not gonna do that.

01:45:22.882 --> 01:45:26.185
Are we going to sanction Russia to the tune of gazillions

01:45:26.185 --> 01:45:28.465
of dollars so that it hurts our economies?

01:45:28.465 --> 01:45:29.555
No, we're not gonna do that."

01:45:29.555 --> 01:45:32.415
So ultimately, we go, "All right, we'll let you get away

01:45:32.415 --> 01:45:36.202
with murdering this dissident on foreign soil."

01:45:37.395 --> 01:45:40.635
And just like what has happened in the Khashoggi murder,

01:45:40.635 --> 01:45:42.223
where essentially they've been able to get away with it

01:45:42.223 --> 01:45:46.688
because of the amount of money in business and investment,

01:45:47.615 --> 01:45:50.295
we're seeing this stretch way beyond governments

01:45:50.295 --> 01:45:54.235
and it's now in these media companies and corporations,

01:45:54.235 --> 01:45:58.225
where a couple months ago, Amazon announced

01:45:58.225 --> 01:46:01.155
that they were acquiring Souk.

01:46:01.155 --> 01:46:05.905
Souk was essentially the Amazon of Saudi Arabia, right?

01:46:05.905 --> 01:46:06.738
Right.

01:46:06.738 --> 01:46:11.095
So, now Amazon owns Souk, so Amazon controls, right?

01:46:12.005 --> 01:46:12.965
Commerce in the Kingdom.

01:46:12.965 --> 01:46:14.195
Commerce in the Kingdom.

01:46:14.195 --> 01:46:15.028
Wow.

01:46:15.028 --> 01:46:17.768
And so ultimately the decision was, okay,

01:46:18.845 --> 01:46:23.845
acquire "The Dissident" or continue on billions of dollars

01:46:24.285 --> 01:46:25.715
of business that we're gonna do in the Kingdom.

01:46:25.715 --> 01:46:26.548
Yeah, it's not-

01:46:26.548 --> 01:46:27.381
We'll do the business in the-

01:46:27.381 --> 01:46:29.395
It's not a difficult economic decision to make

01:46:29.395 --> 01:46:33.015
and it's amazing that we can all rally

01:46:33.015 --> 01:46:37.045
around a free speech issue when it's confronted

01:46:37.045 --> 01:46:42.045
with a government that is trying to clamp down on it

01:46:44.545 --> 01:46:47.025
by way of of laws, right?

01:46:47.025 --> 01:46:50.315
But here we are in the West, where we technically

01:46:50.315 --> 01:46:53.425
have freedom of speech, and yet economic interests

01:46:53.425 --> 01:46:55.985
are creating this unbelievable chilling effect

01:46:55.985 --> 01:46:58.175
on the free exchange of ideas, and it's happening

01:46:58.175 --> 01:46:59.745
right underneath our eyes.

01:46:59.745 --> 01:47:04.745
We're seeing this progressive restriction as a result

01:47:05.195 --> 01:47:07.055
of those economic tectonic plates,

01:47:07.055 --> 01:47:10.415
but also from the people themselves.

01:47:10.415 --> 01:47:13.135
It's like we're policing ourselves when it comes

01:47:13.135 --> 01:47:13.968
to free speech.

01:47:13.968 --> 01:47:17.855
So it's a very different conversation around free speech

01:47:17.855 --> 01:47:21.115
than that, which, we're talking about when we look

01:47:21.115 --> 01:47:23.198
at places like Saudi Arabia.

01:47:24.315 --> 01:47:28.665
I have many friends who will go unnamed,

01:47:28.665 --> 01:47:32.265
that had various projects in development

01:47:32.265 --> 01:47:35.715
with these major media companies that looked at things

01:47:38.568 --> 01:47:42.038
on human rights levels, on political levels,

01:47:44.115 --> 01:47:48.935
on stories such as this and that these global streamers

01:47:48.935 --> 01:47:50.945
have all basically said, "No, we're not gonna do

01:47:50.945 --> 01:47:53.635
these stories anymore."

01:47:53.635 --> 01:47:57.685
I have heard stories of unnamed companies,

01:47:57.685 --> 01:47:59.585
but among these companies that basically have had

01:47:59.585 --> 01:48:02.065
shareholder meetings, or they have discussed

01:48:02.065 --> 01:48:05.555
in these meetings that anything that is political,

01:48:05.555 --> 01:48:10.555
anything that we're to take on the Trump administration,

01:48:12.835 --> 01:48:17.835
or we're to take on China or to go against, you name it,

01:48:18.665 --> 01:48:20.378
we're no longer interested in.

01:48:21.225 --> 01:48:25.645
And the reason being is because the growth in these regions,

01:48:25.645 --> 01:48:29.832
right, is more important than the human rights

01:48:29.832 --> 01:48:31.895
in these regions.

01:48:31.895 --> 01:48:36.065
And so, ultimately, if you can expand, let's say,

01:48:36.065 --> 01:48:39.275
and have millions of subscribers in Saudi Arabia,

01:48:39.275 --> 01:48:43.035
a wealthy country, and have all this revenue

01:48:43.035 --> 01:48:47.835
from Saudi Arabia, versus showing these awful

01:48:47.835 --> 01:48:51.355
human rights abuses in the country, well,

01:48:51.355 --> 01:48:56.035
you're gonna take the money over what is ethics

01:48:56.035 --> 01:49:00.105
and morality, and I think it's been a rude awakening

01:49:00.105 --> 01:49:05.105
to me as a filmmaker and as an activist

01:49:07.465 --> 01:49:11.352
to think that, but this is kind of happening,

01:49:14.345 --> 01:49:19.345
because I view that those who have wealth and power,

01:49:20.185 --> 01:49:24.075
in a perfect world, especially in the Western world,

01:49:24.075 --> 01:49:26.586
should be the ones who are standing up

01:49:26.586 --> 01:49:31.586
and having the strength to shed light for the world,

01:49:32.525 --> 01:49:37.354
into places where people are being oppressed or suppressed.

01:49:37.354 --> 01:49:40.275
And just like you see with the NBA in China,

01:49:40.275 --> 01:49:43.075
or ESPN in China, being able to walk away

01:49:43.075 --> 01:49:45.785
from all these abuses, or what China has been doing

01:49:45.785 --> 01:49:48.205
in Hong Kong, yet, all these companies

01:49:48.205 --> 01:49:50.955
are still doing business with China, despite them trying

01:49:50.955 --> 01:49:55.955
to turn Hong Kong into into a dictatorship, right?

01:49:57.415 --> 01:50:01.255
And yet, nobody's willing to stop doing business with them.

01:50:02.221 --> 01:50:07.221
And we're seeing this across the board,

01:50:07.755 --> 01:50:10.855
and it's disheartening for someone like me,

01:50:10.855 --> 01:50:13.305
who wants to continue to make films like this

01:50:13.305 --> 01:50:16.985
and bring stories like this forward, having to struggle

01:50:18.545 --> 01:50:23.545
to have this content seen, and I would have thought

01:50:27.015 --> 01:50:31.455
that having had the honor of winning an Academy Award

01:50:31.455 --> 01:50:33.115
would have changed that.

01:50:33.115 --> 01:50:36.055
Right, given you a little bit more latitude

01:50:36.055 --> 01:50:39.118
and door swinging wide open at Netflix.

01:50:40.535 --> 01:50:41.488
You would think.

01:50:43.895 --> 01:50:46.365
But that is not the case.

01:50:46.365 --> 01:50:50.145
And then on the other hand, you can see this

01:50:50.145 --> 01:50:52.475
from the company's perspective, right?

01:50:52.475 --> 01:50:55.685
You're watching "The Dissident", how Jeff Bezos

01:50:55.685 --> 01:50:56.858
is able to be hacked.

01:50:57.805 --> 01:50:59.365
You watch how somebody is able

01:50:59.365 --> 01:51:00.975
to be chopped up and murdered.

01:51:00.975 --> 01:51:03.005
So if you're that company, right, on the flip side,

01:51:03.005 --> 01:51:05.145
you can imagine those internal conversations

01:51:05.145 --> 01:51:08.865
of going like, "I don't want them to hack us,

01:51:08.865 --> 01:51:11.055
I don't want them to hack my company,

01:51:11.055 --> 01:51:13.565
I don't want them to take down my servers,

01:51:13.565 --> 01:51:17.835
I don't want them to launch a Twitter campaign against us,

01:51:17.835 --> 01:51:19.485
I don't wanna-"

01:51:19.485 --> 01:51:23.325
Right, and then not that you need to covet favor with MBS,

01:51:23.325 --> 01:51:26.935
but no need to antagonize him when there's so much

01:51:26.935 --> 01:51:30.255
business opportunity to be had potentially in the future.

01:51:30.255 --> 01:51:33.275
Right, and there's so much risk to be had

01:51:33.275 --> 01:51:37.785
by taking that on, and I think

01:51:37.785 --> 01:51:42.435
that that equation ultimately,

01:51:42.435 --> 01:51:45.865
I don't think the word is bad decision making,

01:51:45.865 --> 01:51:49.783
I think leads into risk assessment analysis.

01:51:49.783 --> 01:51:52.205
And each one of these companies or corporations,

01:51:52.205 --> 01:51:55.462
there's risk assessment, and that risk assessment

01:51:55.462 --> 01:51:59.535
announcement is, "Okay, on one hand, whatever,

01:51:59.535 --> 01:52:01.435
hundreds of millions of people will see the film,

01:52:01.435 --> 01:52:03.818
and there'll be accolades and it'll be this, that

01:52:03.818 --> 01:52:05.010
and the other.

01:52:05.010 --> 01:52:08.768
On the other hand, maybe x, y, z can happen.

01:52:09.925 --> 01:52:13.515
Another filmmaker I admire, Ryan White,

01:52:13.515 --> 01:52:18.275
who did "The Keepers", did a film this year

01:52:18.275 --> 01:52:20.875
called "Assassins", and it's on the murder

01:52:20.875 --> 01:52:24.445
of Kim Jong Il's half-brother in Malaysia,

01:52:24.445 --> 01:52:26.625
by the two women that basically poisoned him

01:52:26.625 --> 01:52:27.785
at the airport, right?

01:52:27.785 --> 01:52:29.828
And you, it's North Korea.

01:52:31.095 --> 01:52:36.095
Again, Ryan's film, nobody has stepped up on a global level

01:52:36.165 --> 01:52:37.405
to distribute this.

01:52:37.405 --> 01:52:41.725
It's being distributed by a small distributor, and-

01:52:41.725 --> 01:52:45.615
I've never heard of it.
Yeah, that's a great film.

01:52:45.615 --> 01:52:50.155
And again, he was at Sundance, and the story

01:52:50.155 --> 01:52:51.755
is the exact story of "The Dissident"

01:52:51.755 --> 01:52:54.955
which is, all of these global streamers in here,

01:52:54.955 --> 01:52:57.695
Ryan had had tremendous success with "The Keepers"

01:52:57.695 --> 01:52:59.235
in Netflix, right?

01:52:59.235 --> 01:53:04.235
And ultimately, they went, oh, well, the Sony hack, right,

01:53:04.775 --> 01:53:08.055
was the North Koreans, and that was really embarrassing

01:53:08.055 --> 01:53:10.665
for Sony and this was over film, "The Interview",

01:53:10.665 --> 01:53:12.135
which was a comedy.

01:53:12.135 --> 01:53:14.012
And it's all these global streamers going like,

01:53:14.012 --> 01:53:17.145
okay, they hacked Sony, they messed him up,

01:53:17.145 --> 01:53:19.805
screw it, it doesn't matter that "Assassin"

01:53:19.805 --> 01:53:21.785
is an important film, it doesn't matter

01:53:21.785 --> 01:53:24.575
that it's inside into North Korea, it doesn't matter

01:53:24.575 --> 01:53:28.715
that it shows how what this is, not for us.

01:53:28.715 --> 01:53:30.635
We don't want the risk.

01:53:30.635 --> 01:53:35.635
And so I think there has to be a re-imagining

01:53:35.965 --> 01:53:40.355
for storytellers or activists that want to see to it

01:53:42.815 --> 01:53:45.928
that their content can be globally seen,

01:53:47.467 --> 01:53:50.947
and maybe a new platform is ultimately created

01:53:50.947 --> 01:53:54.105
that allows us, but the counter to this

01:53:54.105 --> 01:53:59.025
is Briarcliff Entertainment, Tom Wartenberg

01:53:59.885 --> 01:54:04.035
who was running Lions Gate and then Open Road

01:54:04.035 --> 01:54:08.315
and did "Spotlight", and did "Crash"

01:54:08.315 --> 01:54:13.315
and did "Fahrenheit 9/11", and has been a real champion

01:54:14.486 --> 01:54:19.486
for difficult films that came forward, about six months ago,

01:54:23.725 --> 01:54:28.355
and acquired the film, and so "The Dissident",

01:54:28.355 --> 01:54:30.915
we planned that it was gonna come out into theaters,

01:54:30.915 --> 01:54:35.915
that it was gonna go on 1,000 screens on October 2nd,

01:54:36.045 --> 01:54:40.705
and of course, with COVID, that wasn't gonna happen.

01:54:40.705 --> 01:54:45.705
So then we changed the plan again to December 18th,

01:54:46.155 --> 01:54:49.565
